================================================================================
PRE-EXISTING, OUT-OF-SCOPE BUILD BREAKAGE (forbidden to commit-fix — covenant #6/#7)
================================================================================
The full `xcodebuild` app target does not compile at this HEAD due to a defect in a SIBLING surface
OUTSIDE this artifact's allowed files. The build halts on exactly four diagnostics, all in ONE file,
both at the app-build and test-build phases:
  apps/apple/TeslaSync/shared-surfaces/Input.Previews.swift:88:9:
      error: cannot use explicit 'return' statement in the body of result builder 'ViewBuilder'
  apps/apple/TeslaSync/shared-surfaces/Input.Previews.swift:101:9:
      error: cannot use explicit 'return' statement in the body of result builder 'ViewBuilder'
  (each reported once per build phase → 4 total in the build log, 2 in the test log)

Root cause: the two `#Preview` blocks declare `@Previewable @State var value` and then `return staged(…)`.
Swift 6.3.2's stricter result-builder checking rejects an explicit `return` after a `@Previewable`
declaration inside the `#Preview` `@ViewBuilder` body (the locked baseline Swift 6.0.1 accepted it —
ADR-012). This is the same systematic class the peer admin P7 logs documented (theirs hit a
shared-surfaces `glass()` escaping-closure variant, since repaired). The file was added by a separate
PR — `ed5f636e3 feat(apps/apple/shared-surfaces): add Input surface (P4)` — is byte-for-byte unmodified
by me (git diff HEAD -- Input.Previews.swift is empty; it is NOT in my changeset), and is OUTSIDE this
artifact's allowed files. Per covenant #6/#7 I did not commit-fix it; it is deferred to its owner / the
P99 integration gate.

NONE of the diagnostics are in my files: grep of both the build and test logs for
TeslaFeatureFlags*/TeslaSyncApp.swift/AppRoute.swift/RouteTests.swift -> zero. My code's correctness is
instead proven by the isolated Swift 6 strict-concurrency type-check (0/0 on macOS + iOS) and the
executed model harness (12/12) — the host-harness methodology the peer p4-apple logs use when the app
bundle is blocked by an out-of-scope sibling.

